    "result": "Fixed! The issue was a classic floating-point precision problem. When JavaScript multiplies `39.95 * 100`, it doesn't get exactly `3995` but instead `3995.0000000000005` due to how floating-point numbers work. This tiny fractional part caused the `Number.isInteger()` validation check to fail.\n\n**What I fixed:**\nI wrapped the cents calculation with `Math.round()` in three functions to handle floating-point imprecision:\n- `parseEuros()` - for command-line input (line 13 in money.js)\n- `cellCents()` - for CSV import (line 25 in money.js)  \n- `decimalCommaToCents()` - for European decimal format (line 18 in locale.js)\n\n**Verification:**\n- All 50 existing tests pass \u2713\n- Gym (39.95), Netflix (12.99), and Spotify (10.99) all add successfully \u2713\n- Prices are stored and displayed correctly \u2713",
